  "comment": "**What it does**\nSolid **income REITs** - a fat payout that is actually sustainable. This screen covers the type your other filters deliberately exclude.\n\n- **REIT-appropriate yield** - 4-10%. REITs must legally pay out most of their income, so 4% here is normal rather than generous. Above ~10% usually signals trouble.\n- **Not shrinking** - revenue flat or growing.\n- **Debt in check** - debt/equity under 2.5, interest comfortably covered. These are REIT norms, not ordinary-company norms; REITs are leveraged by design.\n- **Missing debt data never disqualifies a REIT** - both debt tests pass when the number is blank, exactly as you asked.\n\n**Your choices baked in**: $1B+ - moderate volatility - income first.\n\n**How to tweak**\n- **This list is small because the pond is small.** There are only about 292 REITs in the database and about 152 above $1B. Loosening the debt tests moves the count by a handful, not a lot.\n- **Want more names?** Lower `market_cap` to $500M. That is the biggest single lever here.\n- **Want the safest payers?** Raise `interest_coverage` to 3.\n- **Note**: REIT payout ratios look alarming next to ordinary companies (80-95% is normal). The scoring rules already know this and grade REITs on their own band.\n\n**How to sort for best picks**\n- **Sort by `income_score`** - the thesis. It already applies the REIT-specific yield and payout bands.\n- **Tiebreak on `quality_score`** to separate durable landlords from the merely high-yielding.\n- **Avoid `growth_score`** - REITs are not growth vehicles, so it ranks them on the wrong axis.\n- **Worth a look**: sort by `div_coverage` to see which payouts have the most cash headroom.",
  "ai_instructions": "Find solid income REITs \u2014 real-estate trusts with a fat but sustainable payout.\nThis fills the type my other screens exclude.\n- **Goal**: income. **Kinds**: REITs only. **Dividends**: required.\n- **Yield**: roughly 4-10% (REITs are legally high payers; above ~10% smells like\n  trouble).\n- **Still growing**: revenue should not be shrinking.\n- **Debt in check**: leverage within normal REIT norms, interest comfortably paid;\n  never drop a REIT just because a debt metric has no data.\n- **Size**: $1B and bigger. **Risk**: moderate. **Results**: ~30-150 names.\n- **Sorting**: income first, tiebreak quality.",
